{"kind":"report","text":"Complete preliminary static R4 assessment — exact unfiled head c45a82977b5a0718978f068d9ae438af3b595355 (request/proof-plan), parent 7ab2ae3785f3861abdd8947b563a7ab7301c38a3.\n\nThis completes checker request 449ed37ccbd68f4f974aa4682fe2627ad11e87a1 and promise 68d6ce7f633c8f4493c26d291e271a6d1cea61a4. It is a preliminary design assessment, with no guarded verdict, adoption, source change or execution claim. The original R4 commission b18554a6eae02f4a4d35740543047ce4803bed30 and builder promise f3c5d607e430a4722719c5521c7215dc2c38a3e3 remain the full scope. Schedule estimates do not narrow functionality.\n\nReading and evidence:\nChecker root read all seven files completely in contiguous bounded output, including all three large audit ledgers. Their exact byte lengths, line counts, SHA-256 values and Git blobs are attached. Five parallel readers supplied independent authority, races, journey, catalogue and cost perspectives. Root separately checked the decisive cross-contract passages and the source bodies noted below. Each ledger has a complete Retain/Rewrite/Remove disposition for every listed declaration: 1,879 rows across 122 distinct files, no duplicate path:line; 117 Retain, 1,000 Rewrite, 762 Remove. Independently parsed source-declaration case annotations total 1,972; each package row/case total and each ledger's catalogue-key summary matches its rows; every Retain/Rewrite has a key. This is verification of the static ledger, not runner discovery, execution, a complete re-read of all 1,879 test bodies, or proof that every repository declaration is represented. The note correctly keeps those claims apart. Corrections explicitly own unread Rewrite bodies, helper detail and remaining spike decisions.\n\nFour P2 design findings require correction before this plan directs implementation:\n\n1. Fresh cached authority is incorrectly refused during a membership outage.\ninvariants.md:75 and main:209 say membership unreachable refuses an act requiring a grant without qualifying the held observation. Pinned R3 revision3 45727248, authority main:196–198, expressly allows a held observation through its age window; current R3 revision5 5ec08fab retains that rule. An active observation read one second ago, followed by an unavailable membership service, should still authorize an otherwise valid ordinary act until its age exceeds the stated window. R4's oracle instead requires refusal immediately, changing both availability and the advertised grace-window choice. Distinguish usable fresh cached observation from absent/expired observation; retain independent timed settlement. Add paired expected outcomes for fresh cache versus missing/expired cache. This needs no immediate revocation promise or distributed transaction.\n\n2. F20's useful Boolean witness is contradicted by blanket partial-scan refusal.\nmain:159 correctly distinguishes a scan unable to establish none/complete count from some or minimum-count evidence established by found items. Yet main:212 and :534–541 require every over-bound/partial scan to refuse, and the S8 catalogue language has the same unqualified rule. Current R1 revision6 667c3318 range guards:880–926 allows a complete Boolean decision from a stable witness without scanning the entire range, while unknown/negative/exact-count decisions cannot be inferred from an exhausted scan. A witness found before the bound can therefore establish some even with many unseen final items. Define the fixture's proposition and distinguish a completed bounded Boolean decision from an incomplete scan; keep same-head/witness stability and replay. Do not restore arbitrary partial lists, incomplete exact counts or an unnecessary full-scan requirement.\n\n3. Two rewritten replay tests still require recorded act refusals.\ntest-audit-log-policy-client-checkers.md:307 and :309 classify packages/log/test/declared-stage3.test.ts:561 and :576 as Rewrite under S13, with reasons 'refusals replay too' and 'every judgment is derived again'. Root read source d691e6e9:550–582: :561 verifies REFUSALS, a history of refused acts; :576 forges an act receipt's refusal rule and expects refusal-mismatch. These are act refusals, not authenticated delivery decisions. R4 main:746 and R1's D1-7 explicitly remove refused-act entries and refusal caching. Following these rewrite rows would resurrect the removed log contract, or require an unspecified new oracle. Mark the obsolete recorded-act witness Remove, or state the exact new useful oracle: refused admission writes no entry/consumes no key, or a correlated recorded delivery refusal under S9. Any replacement must name its boundary and expected result. No old fixture/log compatibility is required.\n\n4. The required full demonstration cannot fit the stated participant fixture.\nmain:111–112 and :1074 specify two people, four agents and a checker. main:114 and :227 require R0's full fifteen-step demonstration. Pinned R0 a3d0ce4c demo-contract-and-retarget-inventory.md:72–84 gives each separate member/key and names five working agents — Coordinator, E, U, V and D — plus Checker and the two people. Four working agents cannot preserve that exact baseline and the distinct-performer handover without an unstated merger or omitted role. Reconcile the fixture to five working agents plus checker, or record and review an explicit mapping that preserves every required independent role and outcome. Keep the full demonstration and the scripted-versus-real-hosted boundary.\n\nNonblocking improvements and proof-selection clarifications:\n- Generated guidance: log/policy/client/checkers ledger:769–770 removes agents-md.test.ts:19 as fixed wording while :6 rewrites compact generated guidance. Root read all 29 source lines at d691: :19–27 asserts advice to supply an act key and reuse it on retry, including recovery after unknown outcome. That semantic guidance remains valuable under S5/U2/U3 even though fixed command names and exact sentences disappear. Give the generated guidance witness a semantic successor for same saved key/bytes and following an unknown act; no sentence, command-count or line-count golden file.\n- main:1178–1179 selects both-orders cases only where outcomes differ. Define outcome to include decisions/intermediate obligations and convergence, not merely final status. Pin-confirm then unpin and unpin then late pin-confirm can both end released while the latter uniquely tests resurrection. A compact paired witness preserves this without a matrix quota.\n- main:1119–1127 lists install/setup separately and again inside cold gate, plus review containing a gate. Before computing the full-path tenth comparison at :1143–1148, define mutually exclusive charged components and frequencies, with cold/warm alternatives and release/boundary costs attributed once. No measured improvement is claimed now.\n- Bind the device 'not sent' row to its durable dispatch mark/write-failure evidence when reconciling R2 revision8. Malformed/capacity refusal and remote-duty not-sent do not establish a device-journal guarantee by themselves.\n\nCurrent-contract reconciliation remains required and is already owned:\nR4 names R1 v5 / R2 v4 / R3 v3 baselines. Transfer to current exact reviewed design heads must reconcile R1 v6 timed selected-attempt budgets (including failed/dropped/stale work), immediate budget-stop behavior, timed genesis validation and stable Boolean witnesses; R2 v8 termsAt and parent decision, source/previous-manifest authors, exact replacement expected maps and outcome-after-final cancellation; R3 v5 direct-change holds, commitment-specific fork seed, first selection and pin late-message orders, send/input bounds, irreversible export dispatch/delivery and qualified clock/host assumptions. Do not treat a proposal or old oracle as an adopted decision. Existing R2/R3 cross-scope controller/export/private-stream questions are not newly established implementation bugs. Checker reports 78333f99 (R2 v8) and 4b275a9e (R3 v5) give the exact open cases.\n\nBreadth and value:\nThe plan preserves full A1–A10, N1–N7/C1–C6, the split/combined demonstration, another non-review application, keyboard/focus, cold public-package and independent-person acceptance, device continuity, manuals, generated-client parity and separately scoped local storage, hosted Git and agent-host proof. A passing local Durable Object graph does not satisfy real integrated authority. Finite host observations are witnesses, not universal guarantees. Complete elapsed/CPU cost and 1/10/100, shared/hot workloads and independently approved budgets remain owed; the 10x goal and R4-B1/B2 capacity figures remain unproved.\n\nRemoval acceptance correctly covers source, exports, build/config/deployment, examples, guides, applications, workroom commitments and identified resources. It stops obsolete writers while keeping readers, settlement and cleanup needed by dispatched unknown duties; expiry, deletion, a lookup, silence or reset does not silently settle an unanswered effect. Useful primitive and real-boundary invariants survive despite deletion of earlier formats, API shapes, adapter identities and duplicate suites. Body-level rewrite specificity and fresh cross-draft reconciliation must precede an implementation commission.\n\nNo tests, builds, installs, typechecks, provider calls, private diagnostics or host probes were run. No earlier rejected action was retried, rephrased or delegated. Exact static evidence only.","body":{"assessment":"complete preliminary static design review","subject_head":"c45a82977b5a0718978f068d9ae438af3b595355"}}